    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10369v1</id>
    <title>Sharp Initialization Guarantees for the Heavy-Ball Method</title>
    <updated>2026-10-07T16:36:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10369v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.10369v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The heavy-ball method can accelerate convergence near the minimizer, but it may also enter persistent oscillations on general strongly convex objectives. How should it be initialized to reliably realize this local advantage? We study the initialization neighborhood in which convergence can be guaranteed using only curvature and regularity bounds. This neighborhood shrinks as momentum approaches one; for Lipschitz-continuous Hessians, its radius is of the same order as damping. Matching upper and lower bounds show that this initialization requirement cannot be relaxed uniformly over the function class. We then derive a startup rule based on the current gradient: a finite gradient-descent warm-up enters the guaranteed region before switching to heavy-ball. With parameters optimal for strongly convex quadratics, the subsequent iterates satisfy an accelerated local asymptotic rate bound. Code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/HeavyBall-811D/.</summary>
    <category term="math.OC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-07T16:36:06Z</published>
